𝑵𝒆𝒕𝒘𝒐𝒓𝒌𝒊𝒏𝒈 𝑩𝒂𝒔𝒊𝒄𝒔 𝑬𝒗𝒆𝒓𝒚 𝑫𝒆𝒗𝑶𝒑𝒔 𝑬𝒏𝒈𝒊𝒏𝒆𝒆𝒓 𝒎𝒖𝒔𝒕 𝒌𝒏𝒐𝒘 - Part 1

1. 𝐏𝐮𝐛𝐥𝐢𝐜 𝐈𝐏 𝐕𝐒 𝐏𝐫𝐢𝐯𝐚𝐭𝐞 𝐈𝐏 (Cloud uses both)
  Public IP   -> Internet facing
  Private IP  -> Only inside your network

2. 𝐒𝐮𝐛𝐧𝐞𝐭 𝐚𝐧𝐝 𝐰𝐡𝐲 𝐢𝐭 𝐦𝐚𝐭𝐭𝐞𝐫𝐬?
  Same subnet    = talk directly
  Different subnet  = need routing
   Wrong subnet   = errors

3. 𝐃𝐞𝐟𝐚𝐮𝐥𝐭 𝐆𝐚𝐭𝐞𝐰𝐚𝐲
  Your network's exit door
  without gateway -> traffic wont leave your subnet

4. 𝐃𝐍𝐒 (𝐃𝐨𝐦𝐚𝐢𝐧 𝐍𝐚𝐦𝐞 𝐒𝐲𝐬𝐭𝐞𝐦)
  DNS = Phonebook of the Internet
  Converts names to IPs, No DNS = Nothing loads

5. 𝐍𝐀𝐓 (𝐍𝐞𝐭𝐰𝐨𝐫𝐤 𝐀𝐝𝐝𝐫𝐞𝐬𝐬 𝐓𝐫𝐚𝐧𝐬𝐥𝐚𝐭𝐢𝐨𝐧)
  a technique that allows multiple devices on a private network to share a single public IP address for internet access.
              𝐍𝐀𝐓 = 𝐌𝐢𝐝𝐝𝐥𝐞𝐦𝐚𝐧 
    Private IPs go online using a public IP.

6. 𝐏𝐎𝐑𝐓 = 𝐒𝐞𝐫𝐯𝐢𝐜𝐞 𝐝𝐨𝐨𝐫 𝐧𝐮𝐦𝐛𝐞𝐫

      22    - SSH
      80   - HTTP 
     443   - HTTPS
     3306  - MySQL
     6379  - Redis


Part 2

1. 𝐂𝐈𝐃𝐑 𝐍𝐨𝐭𝐚𝐭𝐢𝐨𝐧 (𝐂𝐥𝐚𝐬𝐬𝐥𝐞𝐬𝐬 𝐈𝐧𝐭𝐞𝐫-𝐃𝐨𝐦𝐚𝐢𝐧 𝐑𝐨𝐮𝐭𝐢𝐧𝐠)
   
   Used everywhere in AWS VPC creation

        /24 -> 256 IPs
        /20 -> 4096 IPs
       /16  -> 65,536 IPs

Quick Formula:

Number of IPs = 2^(32 − CIDR)



2. 𝐑𝐨𝐮𝐭𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐓𝐚𝐛𝐥𝐞𝐬 (𝐝𝐞𝐜𝐢𝐝𝐞𝐬 𝐰𝐡𝐞𝐫𝐞 𝐩𝐚𝐜𝐤𝐞𝐭𝐬 𝐠𝐨) 

  RT = Traffic Map
  Contains rules like : Destination, Target (IGW/NAT/Local), Priority
  
  IGW = internet access for public instances.
  NAT = internet access for private instances.

  Incorrect route = service unavailable.

3. 𝐒𝐞𝐜𝐮𝐫𝐢𝐭𝐲 𝐆𝐫𝐨𝐮𝐩𝐬 = 𝐅𝐢𝐫𝐞𝐰𝐚𝐥𝐥 𝐟𝐨𝐫 𝐢𝐧𝐬𝐭𝐚𝐧𝐜𝐞𝐬

   Allows traffic you permit 
   works on instance level
   Stateful (response auto-allowed)  
                        #“Stateful” =means remembers connection

Wrong SG rule = App wont open.

4. 𝐍𝐀𝐂𝐋𝐬 = 𝐍𝐞𝐭𝐰𝐨𝐫𝐤 𝐀𝐜𝐜𝐞𝐬𝐬 𝐂𝐨𝐧𝐭𝐫𝐨𝐥 𝐋𝐢𝐬𝐭𝐬

  Subnet-level firewall

Controls inbound/outbound for subnets in a VPC.
Stateless (need allow both sides)

Evaluated before Security Group.

5. 𝐏𝐫𝐢𝐯𝐚𝐭𝐞 𝐕𝐬 𝐏𝐮𝐛𝐥𝐢𝐜 𝐒𝐮𝐛𝐧𝐞𝐭

Public Subnet : Connected to internet Gateway( Direct internet access)
Private Subnet : Uses NAT Gateway ( No direct external access)

6. 𝐋𝐨𝐚𝐝 𝐁𝐚𝐥𝐚𝐧𝐜𝐞𝐫

  Distributes traffic across servers.
    Types in AWS : 
      ALB (HTTP/HTTPS)  -> Best for Websites, APIs
      NLB (TCP/UDP)    -> High-speed network traffic

Fixes high load & improves reliability.
